 conditional resampling. The point estimate remained positive, but separation from zero was recalibration-dependent, and the magnitudes are of uncertain, setting-varying size rather than established equal. The two settings used differently constructed exposures (humidity in Sri Lanka only) and were not formally compared.
 
-\noindent Evaluations that benchmark climate against weak comparators or leave non-climate structure unmatched can overstate the decision value of climate data for dengue alerting. Benchmarked against recent surveillance, matched for structure, and judged by calibrated decision-curve net benefit, climate added little robust standalone value. A structurally matched decomposition gave a small, consistently-signed climate increment in both settings (Sri Lanka $+0.0087$, Colombia $+0.0078$) that is not robustly distinguishable from zero on raw predictions and is of uncertain, setting-varying magnitude; in Sri Lanka the matched increment separates from zero only after past-only recalibration ($+0.0157$), and in Colombia the matched increment includes zero once model-development uncertainty is added---so it is not established, transportable evidence of climate benefit. The contribution is primarily methodological---benchmark against recent surveillance, match non-climate structure before attributing value to climate, and report calibration and decision curves across an operationally justified threshold range rather than discrimination against weak comparators. Local, setting-specific evaluation remains necessary.
